    "knowledge_point": "Finding a Vector Equation of a Line Given a Point and Direction Vector",
    "problem": "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q(-3,3,3)$ and parallel to $v=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-5,3⟩.$",
    "worked_solution": "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=q+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $Q$ is $q=⟨-3,3,3⟩.$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨-3,3,3⟩+t⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-5,3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
